---
import "katex/dist/katex.min.css";
import BaseLayout from "@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Figure from "@/components/Figure.astro";
import Lightbox from "@/components/Lightbox.astro";
import Equation from "@/components/Equation.astro";
import clAlpha from "@/assets/coursework/aerolab_cl_alpha.png";
import pllt from "@/assets/coursework/aerolab_pllt_delta.png";
import wave from "@/assets/coursework/wave2d_snapshots.png";
import entry from "@/assets/coursework/entry_trajectory.png";
import heat from "@/assets/coursework/heat_conduction.png";
import clarky from "@/assets/coursework/clarky_cp.png";

const sections = [
  { id: "computational-aero", n: "01", title: "Computational aerodynamics" },
  { id: "numerical-methods", n: "02", title: "Numerical methods" },
  { id: "hypersonics", n: "03", title: "Hypersonics (in progress)" },
  { id: "flight-dynamics", n: "04", title: "Flight dynamics & control" },
  { id: "experimental", n: "05", title: "Experimental" },
  { id: "more", n: "06", title: "More courses" },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Coursework"
  description="Coursework highlights: vortex panel and lifting-line methods, PDE solvers, entry aerothermodynamics, flight dynamics and wind-tunnel and heat-transfer labs."
>
  <section class="wrap pt-12 pb-6 md:pt-16">
    <p class="eyebrow">Coursework</p>
    <h1 class="mt-3 text-[clamp(2rem,4.5vw,3rem)]">Coursework highlights</h1>
    <p class="mt-4 max-w-[64ch] text-lg text-muted">
      A figure and a few sentences for each, grouped by theme. These are graded assignments, so the source isn't
      published, but it's available on request. Every figure was regenerated for this page from my course code, in the
      site's figure style.
    </p>
    <nav aria-label="On this page" class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap gap-2">
      {sections.map((s) => (
        <a class="chip no-underline hover:text-ink" href={`#${s.id}`}>
          {s.title}
        </a>
      ))}
    </nav>
  </section>

  <div class="wrap">
    <section id="computational-aero" class="cw-section">
      <h2>
        <span>01</span> Computational aerodynamics
      </h2>
      <div class="cw-grid">
        <div class="prose">
          <h3>ASEN 3802: vortex panel method and lifting-line theory</h3>
          <p>
            A team lab (four students). We built a NACA 4-digit airfoil generator, a Kuethe–Chow constant-strength
            vortex panel method with the Kutta condition, and a Prandtl lifting-line solver using odd Fourier terms. The
            panel method shows what thin-airfoil theory leaves out: thickness raises the lift slope above 2π, and camber
            shifts the zero-lift angle. This is the most direct CFD precursor in my coursework.
            <!-- TODO(Lilo): which tasks were yours? -->
          </p>
          <p class="text-sm text-muted">
            To regenerate the figure I reordered the airfoil points clockwise from the trailing edge, which the archived
            panel routine expects. With that ordering it gives c<sub>l,α</sub> = 0.120/deg for NACA 0012 (thin-airfoil
            theory: 0.110/deg) and c<sub>l</sub> = 0.26 at α = 0 for NACA 2412.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <Figure
            src={clAlpha}
            alt="Sectional lift coefficient against angle of attack from minus 8 to 12 degrees for NACA 0006, 0012, 0018 and 2412 from the vortex panel method, with the thin-airfoil line 2 pi alpha. Thicker sections have slightly steeper slopes; the cambered 2412 is shifted up by about 0.26."
            source="courses/ASEN3802/aero-lab (Vortex_Panel.m, NACAGenerator.m)"
          >
            Vortex panel method, 100 panels per surface, against thin-airfoil theory.
          </Figure>
          <Figure
            src={pllt}
            alt="Induced-drag factor delta against taper ratio from 0 to 1 for aspect ratios 4, 6, 8 and 10; each curve has a minimum near a taper ratio of 0.35 to 0.4 and rises toward both ends."
            source="courses/ASEN3802/aero-lab (PLLT.m)"
          >
            Lifting-line theory: δ = 1/e − 1 for untwisted tapered wings. The minimum near λ ≈ 0.35–0.4 is the classic
            result.
          </Figure>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="numerical-methods" class="cw-section">
      <h2>
        <span>02</span> Numerical methods
      </h2>
      <div class="cw-grid">
        <div class="prose">
          <h3>APPM 3050: Scientific Computing</h3>
          <p>
            Root finding (bisection, Newton, secant), Taylor-series error, Euler, RK2 and RK4 integrators, an iterative
            finite-difference boundary-value problem, a Monte Carlo random-walk escape probability, and explicit
            finite-difference solvers for the 2-D wave and heat equations. Two projects grew into full pages:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <a href="/projects/tsunami-simulation/">2-D tsunami over variable bathymetry</a> (with animation)
            </li>
            <li>
              <a href="/projects/target-shooting/">Shooting method with event detection</a>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <Figure
          src={wave}
          alt="Four 3D snapshots of a vibrating square membrane from t = 0 to about 1, starting as a single positive dome, flattening, inverting and returning."
          source="courses/APPM3050/wave-2d (scheme re-run, vectorised)"
        >
          2-D wave equation on a fixed-edge square membrane, explicit central differences, λ = (cΔt/Δx)² = 0.25.
        </Figure>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="hypersonics" class="cw-section">
      <h2>
        <span>03</span> Hypersonics (in progress)
      </h2>
      <div class="cw-grid">
        <div class="prose">
          <h3>ASEN 5131: Hypersonic Aerodynamics (graduate)</h3>
          <p>
            Homework 1 compared the exponential atmosphere with the 1976 US Standard Atmosphere, then integrated a
            ballistic entry at 4.5 km/s and −70° (3,400 kg, C<sub>D</sub> = 0.4, 5 cm nose radius) three ways: the
            Allen–Eggers analytic solution, the numerically integrated ballistic equations, and the full equations with
            gravity and curvature. Stagnation heating uses Sutton–Graves.
          </p>
          <Equation
            tex={String.raw`\frac{V}{V_0} = \exp\!\left(-\frac{\rho}{\beta}\right),\quad \beta = -\frac{2m\alpha\sin\gamma_0}{C_D A},\qquad \dot q = K\sqrt{\rho}\,V^3`}
          />
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th></th>
                <th>Analytic</th>
                <th>Ballistic (numerical)</th>
                <th>Full equations</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td>Peak deceleration</td>
                <td class="num">49.17 g</td>
                <td class="num">49.18 g</td>
                <td class="num">53.30 g (+8.4 %)</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Altitude at peak deceleration</td>
                <td class="num">7.55 km</td>
                <td class="num">7.55 km</td>
                <td class="num">7.40 km</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Peak heat flux</td>
                <td class="num">18.77 MW/m²</td>
                <td class="num">18.77 MW/m²</td>
                <td class="num">21.24 MW/m² (+13.1 %)</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Altitude at peak heating</td>
                <td class="num">15.53 km</td>
                <td class="num">15.53 km</td>
                <td class="num">15.30 km</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
          <p class="text-sm text-muted">
            The numerical ballistic solution matches the analytic peaks to within 0.02 %, which verifies the integrator.
            Adding gravity raises peak heating by 13 %. My <a href="/projects/hypersonic-solver/">hypersonic solver</a>{" "}
            picks up the same problem at the boundary-layer level.
          </p>
        </div>
        <Figure
          src={entry}
          alt="Left, altitude against velocity for ballistic and full-equation entries with analytic peak-deceleration and peak-heating points on the ballistic curve. Right, Sutton-Graves stagnation heat flux against altitude peaking near 15 km at about 19 and 21 MW per square metre."
          source="courses/ASEN5131/HW1 (re-run)"
        >
          Ballistic entry: altitude–velocity and stagnation heating. Markers are the analytic peaks.
        </Figure>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="flight-dynamics" class="cw-section">
      <h2>
        <span>04</span> Flight dynamics & control
      </h2>
      <div class="cw-grid">
        <div class="prose">
          <h3>ASEN 3801: Aircraft Dynamics</h3>
          <p>
            Lab 4 was a quadrotor: full 12-state nonlinear equations of motion, a motor-to-force/moment mixing matrix, a
            linearised model, and inner-loop rate feedback plus outer-loop velocity feedback, compared in simulation
            (team lab). Lab 5 extended the equations of motion to a fixed-wing aircraft with aerodynamic forces and
            moments. Earlier labs covered rotation matrices and Euler angles from experimental tracking data.
            <!-- TODO(Lilo): your part of lab 4; a figure from your runs would help. -->
          </p>
          <p>
            The same ideas, at a larger scale, are in <a href="/projects/sim6dof/">Sim6DOF</a>.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card p-5">
          <p class="mono-label">Quadrotor equations of motion (lab 4)</p>
          <Equation
            tex={String.raw`\dot{\boldsymbol\omega} = I^{-1}\big(\mathbf G_c - \boldsymbol\omega \times I\boldsymbol\omega\big),\qquad \dot{\mathbf v}^B = \mathbf v^B\times\boldsymbol\omega + R^{\mathsf T}\mathbf g + \tfrac{1}{m}\big(\mathbf F_\text{aero} + \mathbf F_c\big)`}
          />
          <p class="text-sm text-muted">
            The control forces and moments come from the four rotor thrusts through a mixing matrix. The figure is still
            to come.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="experimental" class="cw-section">
      <h2>
        <span>05</span> Experimental
      </h2>
      <div class="cw-grid">
        <div class="prose">
          <h3>ASEN 3802: heat conduction in metal rods</h3>
          <p>
            Eight thermocouples along aluminium, brass and steel rods heated at one end. We compared the measured
            temperature histories with an analytic Fourier-series solution, including a convergence study in the number
            of modes. The end-of-test gradient separates the materials clearly: about 287 K/m for steel at 22 V, against
            79 K/m for aluminium at 30 V, even though the steel heater ran at lower power.
          </p>
          <h3>ASEN 2802: Clark Y-14 wind-tunnel test</h3>
          <p>
            Surface-pressure ports on a Clark Y-14 wing in the wind tunnel, reduced to pressure coefficient and
            integrated for sectional lift and drag, plus a hot-air-balloon altitude and density model.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <Figure
            src={heat}
            alt="Left, eight thermocouple temperature histories for an aluminium rod at 25 V, rising over about 25 minutes to steady values between 19 and 24 degrees C. Right, end-of-test temperature against position for five rod and voltage cases, each with a straight-line fit; steel has the steepest slope."
            source="courses/ASEN3802/heat-conduction-lab (raw thermocouple data)"
          >
            Measured thermocouple data and end-of-test profiles with linear fits.
          </Figure>
          <Figure
            src={clarky}
            alt="Pressure coefficient against chordwise position for a Clark Y-14 at 10 degrees angle of attack: a suction peak near minus 2.6 close to the leading edge on one surface, and near-zero to slightly positive values on the other."
            source="courses/ASEN2802/milestone3/aero (FullSpanTest.csv)"
          >
            Clark Y-14 surface pressure at α = 10° and 29.9 m/s, at a mid-span station.
          </Figure>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="more" class="cw-section">
      <h2>
        <span>06</span> More courses
      </h2>
      <ul class="grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3">
        <li class="card p-4">
          <p class="font-medium">ASEN 4018 Senior Design</p>
          <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-muted">
            The capstone weight model, flap model and design trades, and the 6-DOF simulator. See the{" "}
            <a class="text-flow underline" href="/projects/aircraft-design-toolbox/">
              capstone weight-model page
            </a>
            .
          </p>
        </li>
        <li class="card p-4">
          <p class="font-medium">ATLS 5151 Flow Visualization (graduate)</p>
          <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-muted">
            Marangoni flows, Chladni patterns and clouds. See the{" "}
            <a class="text-flow underline" href="/flow-vis/">
              gallery
            </a>
            .
          </p>
        </li>
        <li class="card p-4">
          <p class="font-medium">ASEN 3200 Orbital Mechanics</p>
          <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-muted">Orbital mechanics homework, the background for DebriSolver.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="card p-4">
          <p class="font-medium">ASEN 3711 / 3712 / 3713</p>
          <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-muted">Aerodynamics, structures, and thermodynamics &amp; heat transfer.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="card p-4">
          <p class="font-medium">ASEN 3300 Aerospace Electronics</p>
          <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-muted">Circuits labs and Arduino sensor work.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="card p-4">
          <p class="font-medium">APPM 2360 · ASEN 2012 · CSCI 1300</p>
          <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-muted">
            Differential equations, experimental and computational methods, and C++ programming.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </div>
  <Lightbox />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .cw-section {
    padding-block: 3.5rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
    scroll-margin-top: 5rem;
  }
  .cw-section > h2 {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    font-size: var(--text-2xl);
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
  }
  .cw-section > h2 span {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    color: var(--text-faint);
  }
  .cw-grid {
    display: grid;
    gap: 2rem;
  }
  .cw-grid > * {
    min-width: 0;
  }
  @media (min-width: 1024px) {
    .cw-grid {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1.05fr);
      gap: 3rem;
      align-items: start;
    }
  }
  .cw-grid :global(.figure) {
    margin-top: 0;
  }
  .cw-grid .prose h3:first-child {
    margin-top: 0;
  }
</style>
